to-david-spivak-source : List Word
to-david-spivak-source = lines-of (cons "# David Spivak"
  (cons ""
  (cons "David, you lead the Collective Intelligence group at the Topos Institute. Its question, as your program states it, is how we make sense of the world together. Avik Jain's paper *hyperactivity* is subtitled a decentralized operating system language for collective superintelligence, and its account of that phrase is exact, reusable reasoning made available through interaction: established conclusions retain the evidence and dependencies that govern their use"
  (cons (cite s1-3-p4 "(§1.3)")
  (cons ". This page sets out what in your work the paper meets, and why your work matters more than the field around it has recognized. Each claim about the work links to the block that states it. Every piece of your work the system builds on is credited to you in full."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## The interface is the object"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Polynomial functors carry a claim the field still treats as a technique: a system is its interface. A system has positions it can occupy and, at each position, directions it can receive. Wiring, composition, dynamics and decision all follow from that one shape. Machine learning designs architectures and treats the interface as fixed scaffolding around the thing that learns. Your work turns this around: the interface is where the meaning is, and interaction is what happens across it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "The February 2026 paper goes one step past the theory you built with Nelson Niu. In *Interactions that reshape the interfaces of the interacting parties*, each round of interaction, an output chosen and an input received, determines the next interface. The examples are a differentiating cell, a sensor that loses a channel, and a generator whose resolution grows when the gradient says it is ready. Polynomial trees are elements of the terminal (u ◁ u)-coalgebra. PolyTr is monoidal closed. OrgTr generalizes Org, and Org embeds in it locally fully faithfully as the constant trees. Learning, development and conversation all change what the parties can say and hear. A theory of collective intelligence that keeps interfaces fixed describes everything except intelligence."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Where the paper meets it"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The paper's processes are built over your shape. A command signature is a command type with its dependent type of responses"
  (cons (cite s7-6-p2 "(§7.6)")
  (cons ": positions, and directions at each position. A continuing process over it returns, takes a silent delayed step, or issues a command with a response-indexed delayed continuation, a guarded fixed point in clocked cubical type theory"
  (cons (cite s7-6-p19 "(§7.6)")
  (cons ". A handler interprets each command as a process over another signature, and induction constructs the paths witnessing the identity and composition laws for handlers"
  (cons (cite s7-6-p11 "(§7.6)")
  (cons ". A composed fleet is one process over its remaining environment"
  (cons (cite s7-6-p15 "(§7.6)")
  (cons ", as in Org."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Your reshaping interfaces are a further step the paper does not take as a construction: polynomial trees are coinductive objects, and the clocked guarded fixed point is the form in which the paper writes coinductive behaviour"
  (cons (cite s7-6-p17 "(§7.6)")
  (cons ". The paper says how a changed representation relates to the old one. An observation that forgets information, a refinement of behavior, and an equivalence of complete structures have different types and consequences"
  (cons (cite s1-p27 "(§1)")
  (cons ", and where the change is an equivalence of structures, transport carries operations and proofs across it"
  (cons (cite s4-p17 "(§4)")
  (cons ". An interpretation can itself change across inputs: one input may change the bindings or clauses used for the next"
  (cons (cite s6-3-p13 "(§6.3)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sense-making with accounts attached"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Your Accountable Sense-making track asks for a mathematics of dynamical systems, decision processes, information pipelines and working languages in which sense-making can be accounted for. The paper's account of knowledge graphs is built on that requirement: an edge is a term inhabiting the type of the relation it establishes, so an edge that inhabits its type is its justification"
  (cons (cite hyperactivity-is-the-theory-of-knowledge-graphs-source "(Part III, hyperactivity as the theory of knowledge graphs)")
  (cons (cite s1-p25 "(§1, 1.5)")
  (cons ". Definitions live in a content-addressed codebase with their derivations and dependency certificates"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p6 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ", and a rendering is a chosen observation of the retained objects"
  (cons (cite s10-9-p6 "(§10.9)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "What an observation warrants is a construction over the fiber of histories compatible with it"
  (cons (cite s1-p12 "(1.3)")
  (cons ", and refining the observation keeps what was established"
  (cons (cite s10-4-p6 "(§10.4)")
  (cons ". A law on the whole yields a law on what is observed only when it descends through the observation"
  (cons (cite s1-p16 "(§1, a reversible whole with no observed law)")
  (cons ". For language, an occurrence's meaning is the family of continuations transported from the past occurrences its context selects, determinate exactly when that family is contractible"
  (cons (cite language-derived-from-the-record-source "(Part III, Language derived from the record)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Collective intelligence"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Avik's question to this project was whether the collective intelligence effect is understood. The empirical fact, from Woolley and colleagues, is that a group's intelligence is not predicted by its members' average or best individual intelligence. It is predicted by how evenly the members take turns and how well they read one another. Your framework says why: intelligence is in the wiring, the interfaces and their compositions, not in the nodes."
  (cons ""
  (cons "The paper states the same thing in its own terms. Combining observations constrains a joint fiber; a participant can use another's observation only when its interaction supplies the relevant information; no global knower and no separate theory of knowledge is required"
  (cons (cite s10-4-p8 "(§10.4)")
  (cons ". Two people who disagree hold two constructions, and the system can show the shared premises and the exact point where their inferences part, so disagreement becomes navigable"
  (cons (cite hyperactivity-is-the-theory-of-knowledge-graphs-source "(Part III, Communication)")
  (cons ". Your reshaping interfaces add the dynamic half: when the parties talk, what they can say and hear changes."
  (cons ""
  (cons "That intention is concrete. Part III closes its account of the people whose work it composes with the work's next step: all of them in one room"
  (cons (cite metacircular-univalent-unison-of-cubically-receptive-stateboxes-source "(Part III, Whose shoulders)")
  (cons ". Avik is gathering them, from type theory, categorical semantics, cryptography, distributed systems, knowledge representation and human-compatible AI, into one shared thread rather than separate conversations. You have spent a decade on the mathematics of what such a thread is. The invitation is to be in it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What your work is owed"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The field treats applied category theory as a specialty within mathematics. Your program treats it as the working language of cooperation, and the progressive-GAN example shows why that is right: the design decision machine learning makes by hand, when to grow the interface, is in your framework a morphism driven by feedback. Polynomial interfaces, organizations whose wiring changes, and sense-making that carries its accounts are yours, and the credit for them is yours."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, the blocks linked above"
  (cons "- Interactions that reshape the interfaces of the interacting parties, arXiv 2602.17917, https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.17917v1"
  (cons "- David Spivak, Topos Institute, https://topos.institute/people/david-spivak/"
  (cons "- Collective intelligence, Topos Institute, https://topos.institute/work/collective-intelligence/"
  (cons "- Niu and Spivak, Polynomial Functors: A Mathematical Theory of Interaction, Cambridge University Press, https://prod-drupal.cambridge.org/cl/universitypress/subjects/mathematics/logic-categories-and-sets/polynomial-functors-mathematical-theory-interaction"
  (cons "- dspivak.net, https://dspivak.net/"
  nil)))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
